# crates/elohim-sdk — Rust client SDK

The [application lifecycle composition map](../../.epr-meta/composition.md#application-lifecycle--authoritative-homes-and-composition)
and [SDK contract home](../../elohim/sdk/.epr-meta) also govern the Rust client surface.
Mirror the shared working-version, sync-state and publication semantics as they are
implemented; do not fork their vocabulary or make HTTP availability a prerequisite
for work the caller can keep locally. This crate remains a client, not a replication
server or authority engine. Proposed working-version verbs are a target contract,
not a claim about today's API. Common conformance cases should exercise both client
implementations; runtime-specific persistence stays an explicit adapter capability.

What belongs here: the client (`src/client`), its cache, reach and sync helpers, the traits a
consumer implements, and the tests that pin its documented behaviour. The README's "Rough
edges" section is part of the contract: a change that alters one of those edges updates the
README in the same pass.

Two structural facts keep the crate honest without an edit-time rule. Every path dependency also
carries a `version`, so the crate stays publishable. The wire types come from `elohim-views`
rather than from `elohim-storage` (the `deny.toml` boundary), so a consumer never pulls in the
storage implementation.
